{"season":2024,"team":"TEN","distance":0.5151,"shared":15,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2018,"team":"BUF","distance":0.668,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"DAL","distance":0.6737,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"CLE","distance":0.6761,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"SF","distance":0.6854,"shared":18,"play_caller":"DeMeco Ryans"},{"season":2019,"team":"CIN","distance":0.7196,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Commanders went 7-10 in 2021 and were outscored 434 to 335. The offense ranked 21st in EPA per play and the defense 26th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. Their most distinctive traits were the 3rd highest 11 personnel usage and the fewest linebackers on the field in the league.","offense":"The Commanders offense was a little below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 3rd highest 11 personnel usage in the league. Taylor Heinicke ran the passing game, with Terry McLaurin leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Commanders defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 11th and 27th. Montez Sweat was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Jonathan Allen, who had 9 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. In terms of scheme, they had the fewest linebackers on the field in the league.","special":"The Commanders were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 85% of field goals, which was about average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the offensive line, about 24% of the money on the books.","players":"Taylor Heinicke played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,419 yards. Terry McLaurin was the clear top target with 24% of the looks and 1,053 receiving yards. Antonio Gibson handled most of the work in the backfield with 62% of the carries. On defense, Jonathan Allen led the team with 9 sacks and Bobby McCain picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2019 Steelers and the 2025 Buccaneers under Josh Grizzard. Their defense looked most like the 2018 Bills and the 2025 Cowboys.","trends":"The offense went from 29th in EPA per play in 2020 to 21st in 2021, a big step forward. The defense moved from 3rd in EPA allowed per play in 2020 to 26th in 2021, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was base defense usage, which went from 31% to 16%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2021. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}